Subject: Convergence issues with goodness-of-fit Pearson residual parameters
Message-ID: <n3ByH1Ry0fY.yyyRjA-AAAAJ@hmecology.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/hmecology/c/n3ByH1Ry0fY/m/yyyRjA-AAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I am modeling reptile communities using JAGS, following Kery & Royle's AHM book. I'm in the final stages of creating the model, with adding the code to calculate goodness-of-fit as is described in chapter 5 and in Wearn et al. 2017.

The model runs fine, but I just cannot get the squared Pearson chi-square residual parameter for each species and site to converge.

To get good mixing for my model without the goodness-of-fit I just adjusted the priors and the initial values, but I can't see what I can change in the goodness-of-fit code. Increasing the iterations has helped a little, but it currently takes ~15 hours to run 800 000 iterations so I'd like to know if there's anything else I can adjust before upping the iterations again.

for(k in 1:nspec){          # Loop over species
    for (i in 1:nsite) {         # Loop over sites
    ynew[i,k] ~ dbin(mup[i,k], J[i]) # Simulate an ‘ideal’ dataset
    # Pearson residuals (A small non-zero value, e, prevents division by zero)
    d[i,k]<- (ysum[i,k] - mup[i,k]*J[i])/sqrt((mup[i,k]+e)*J[i]*(1-mup[i,k]-e))
    d2[i,k]<- pow(d[i,k],2)                            # observed
    dnew[i,k]<- (ynew[i,k]-mup[i,k]*J[i])/sqrt((mup[i,k]+e)*J[i]*(1-mup[i,k]-e))
    dnew2[i,k]<- pow(dnew[i,k],2)                        # expected
    }
    }

d2 is the parameter that won't converge for a number of species and sites. The non-convergence doesn't seem to be due to a specific species or site.

Any ideas on how to get good mixing would be appreciated!

Alexandra

From ucaaac2dc0aae28b8 at groups.google.com  Mon Jul  2 19:10:37 2018
From: ucaaac2dc0aae28b8 at groups.google.com (eric.d.stolen)
Date: Mon, 02 Jul 2018 19:10:37 +0000
Subject: BUGS code for Chandler & Royle (2013)
Message-ID: <o3RZ_Hg-KfQ.HQNoh52dAAAJ@hmecology.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/hmecology/c/o3RZ_Hg-KfQ/m/HQNoh52dAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Has anyone written any BUGS code to implement  the models in Chandler & Royle (2013). "Spatially explicit models for inference about density in unmarked or partially marked populations". The Annals of Applied Statistics 7:936-954?

I have code working for the aggregated count version of the model (below), but I'm having trouble figuring out how to write the version which includes the latent encounter data z[i,j,k] | w[i]. This is necessary to incorporate information on individual identities.

Thanks,
Eric

 model {

    # priors
    sigma ~ dnorm(250,1/50)I(0,)
    lambda.0 ~ dbeta(1,1)
    psi ~ dbeta(1,1)

    for(i in 1:M) {
    w[i] ~ dbern(psi)
    S[i,1] ~ dunif(xlim[1],xlim[2])
    S[i,2] ~ dunif(ylim[1],ylim[2])

    for(j in 1:J) {
    d2[i,j]<- (S[i,1] - X[j,1])^2 + (S[i,2] - X[j,2])^2
    lambda.ij[i,j]<- exp(-d2[i,j]/(2*sigma^2))*w[i]

    }
    }

    for(j in 1:J) {
    lambda.j[j]<- lambda.0*sum(lambda.ij[,j])
    n[j] ~ dpois(K*lambda.j[j])
    }

    N<- sum(w)
    }

